## 9. Architecture and Code Quality

Maintain a clean, scalable project architecture.

Prefer the existing folder conventions when they are reasonable. Otherwise, use a structure similar to:

- `src/app/` — App Router pages, layouts, and route handlers.
- `src/components/` — Reusable UI components.
- `src/components/ui/` — Shared UI primitives.
- `src/features/portfolio/` — Portfolio-related components and logic.
- `src/features/admin/` — Admin-specific components.
- `src/lib/` — Shared utilities, validation, authentication, and helpers.
- `src/db/` — SQLite connection, Drizzle schema, and database utilities.
- `src/db/schema/` — Database schema definitions.
- `src/db/migrations/` — Generated database migrations.
- `src/server/` — Server-only business logic and data access.
- `src/server/services/` — Reusable application services.
- `src/server/repositories/` — Database access abstractions where beneficial.
- `src/server/validators/` — Server-side schemas and validation.
- `public/` — Public static assets.
- `scripts/` — Database initialization and maintenance scripts.

Use a practical architecture rather than creating unnecessary abstractions.

### Coding standards

- Use TypeScript strict mode.
- Avoid `any` unless genuinely necessary and explicitly justified.
- Prefer reusable components over duplicated markup.
- Separate UI, validation, business logic, and database access.
- Keep database operations on the server.
- Use Server Components by default.
- Add Client Components only when client-side interactivity is needed.
- Use Server Actions or Route Handlers appropriately.
- Never expose database connections or server-only modules to client bundles.
- Use consistent naming conventions and clear types.
- Add error handling and structured logging.
- Avoid unnecessary dependencies.
- Remove dead code only after verifying it is genuinely unused.
- Avoid unnecessary refactoring of working parts of the project.

